# Cartwright project rules

This codebase is a Cartwright-engine store. The full project briefing is in `.claude/CLAUDE.md` — read it for context, mode definitions, and the file map.

> **Designing this site? Read `DESIGN.md` first** — the design playbook: the three design
> paths (compose a look / mockup-first / Blank Canvas), built-ins inventory (three.js is
> already shipped — don't install it), taste rules, and screenshot self-verification.

## Run it & sign in (first time)

> **`--profile site` scaffold?** (`.cartwright/profile.json` says which profile you have.) There is no database, admin or login here — skip the first-run steps below: `pnpm dev` is the whole first run, `pnpm build` the whole production gate, and deploying needs no environment variables. Guide: `docs/simple-site.md`.

A fresh project is empty until the schema is created and the admin is seeded. `npx create-cartwright` runs steps 1–2 for you and prints the admin login — if you scaffolded with it, skip to step 3. Manual clone:

1. `pnpm install`
2. `pnpm db:setup` — creates the schema **and** seeds the admin + demo data; prints the admin **email + password** and writes them to **`.admin-credentials`** (gitignored). Auto-falls-back via the libSQL client if `prisma db push` hits the flaky Prisma 7.8 `Schema engine error:`.
3. `pnpm dev` (an agent: `pnpm dev:agent`, see "Start the dev server (agents)") → open **`/account/login`** → **Password** tab. Email = `brand.emails.admin` (from `brand.config.ts`); password = the value in `.admin-credentials` (`cat .admin-credentials`). First login forces a change at `/admin/konto`, then `/admin/setup` opens.

Magic-link only appears once `RESEND_API_KEY` is set (dev link → `.mail-previews/`); until then password is the only method, by design. Pre-set with `ADMIN_PASSWORD` before `db:setup`.

To go from booted to **designed** (apply a Voice/Skin via the REST tool surface, terminal-only), follow `.claude/CLAUDE.md` → "Your first 10 minutes".

## Start the dev server (agents)

Never assume `localhost:3000`: when port 3000 is taken, `next dev` moves on to 3001, 3002, ….
`pnpm dev:agent` is `pnpm dev` plus the URL Next actually chose, written with your
`brand.defaultLocale` path to `.cartwright/dev-url` (e.g. `http://localhost:3001/en`), and the PID
of that `next dev`, written to `.cartwright/dev-pid`. Next allows one `next dev` per project, so
while that PID is alive the block reuses its server and never starts a second one — even when the
URL does not answer yet (a compile, a restart). Only when the PID is dead or missing (a hard kill
leaves both files behind, and another app may take the port) does it delete both files and start
`pnpm dev:agent` in the background. Either way it polls the URL for a 200 with a bound — a cold
first compile takes 10–30 s:

```bash
SERVER_PID=$(cat .cartwright/dev-pid 2>/dev/null)  # the next dev an earlier call started
if ! kill -0 "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null; then         # dead or missing: start a fresh one
  rm -f .cartwright/dev-url .cartwright/dev-pid      # a hard kill leaves both behind
  pnpm dev:agent > /tmp/cartwright-dev.log 2>&1 &
  DEV_PID=$!                                         # started here: yours to kill when done
  SERVER_PID=$DEV_PID
fi                                                   # alive: reuse it, even before it answers
for i in $(seq 90); do                               # bounded: 90 tries, then read the log
  kill -0 "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || break         # it exited: see below
  URL=$(cat .cartwright/dev-url 2>/dev/null) &&
    [ "$(curl -sL -m 60 -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$URL")" = 200 ] && break
  sleep 2
done
BASE=$(sed -E 's#^(https?://[^/]+).*#\1#' .cartwright/dev-url 2>/dev/null)  # e.g. http://localhost:3001
```

Every curl example in these rules uses `$BASE`. No 200 when the loop ends? If the block started the
server, read `/tmp/cartwright-dev.log`: "Another next dev server is already running" means one is up
that the block did not find (a plain `pnpm dev` writes no `.cartwright/dev-url`) — reuse the URL it
prints, or `kill` the PID it names. If it reused one, run the block again: it keeps waiting while
that PID lives and starts a fresh server once it is gone. (After a reboot another process can hold
that PID: if `ps -o args= -p "$(cat .cartwright/dev-pid)"` shows no `next dev`, delete both
`.cartwright/` files first.) Done? If the block started the server, `kill "$DEV_PID"` stops
`next dev` too and deletes both `.cartwright/` files; a server it reused is left running. If your
shell does not keep variables between calls, run the block again in each call — while its server
lives it only reuses it — or start `pnpm dev:agent` once with your tool's own background mode and
re-read `.cartwright/dev-url` in each call.

## Modern web platform first

When choosing between native browser APIs and JS libraries, default to native:

- **Modals/drawers** → `<dialog>` + Popover API (`popover="auto"` / `popover="manual"`), not React-state-only modals.
- **SPA transitions** → `document.startViewTransition` via `app/lib/view-transitions.ts` (when present).
- **Cross-document transitions** → `@view-transition { navigation: auto }` in global CSS.
- **Responsive components** → container queries (`@container`, `container-type: inline-size`), not media-query breakpoints on every component.
- **State-based selectors** → `:has()`, `:user-valid`, `:user-invalid`, `:state(...)`.
- **Animation** → CSS-only where possible (`@starting-style`). JS animation only when CSS can't express it. For site-wide motion use the 3 blessed paths in `.claude/CLAUDE.md` → "Motion & animation": `motionEffects` presets (scroll-driven CSS), the `plugins/three-scenes/` Live Canvas, or the per-project GSAP recipe (SSR-safe + reduced-motion-guarded; gsap is not an engine dependency).
- **Task scheduling** → `scheduler.yield()` for long synchronous work that risks INP regression.
- **Color** → `oklch()` / `lch()` for new palette decisions (existing `--color-sol-*` hex tokens stay — don't rename them).

## Structured data is mandatory

Every page that can be cited by AI search ships `<JsonLd>` markup (SSR only). Helper at `components/JsonLd.tsx`. Currently shipped: Organization, Product, Offer, BreadcrumbList, AggregateRating, BlogPosting, FAQPage, and SoftwareApplication (Cartwright product schema on `/built-with-cartwright`, gated by `brand.features.cartwrightBadge`). Don't put JSON-LD in client components.

## Feature flags

Subsystems live behind `brand.features.*` in `brand.config.ts`. New features ship default-off. Flip on staging, then promote. Don't introduce breaking UI behavior on a default-on flag without a fallback path.

## File map

- `brand.config.ts` — single source of truth for brand, mode, flags, policies.
- `themes/<slug>.css` — color palette per shop.
- `components/JsonLd.tsx` — structured data.
- `lib/feature-flags/manifest.ts` — every feature flag (compile-enforced).
- `lib/plugins/` + `plugins/` — plugin contract (`cartwright-plugin-v1`) + in-repo plugins (first: `plugins/phone-widget/`).
- `app/admin/` — admin UI.
- `lib/ai/prompts/<slug>.ts` — AI assistant prompt per shop.
- `designs/blank/` — the **Blank Canvas** pack: rewrite its header/footer/homepage freely for a fully unique design while all backend keeps working. Guide: `AGENTS.md` → "Blank canvas — build a design from scratch".

## Capturing leads

Don't build an inquiry table — one ships. `Lead` (`prisma/schema.prisma`) ←
`POST /api/inquiries` → `/admin/leads`; `app/actions/lead.ts` for server
actions (built-in fields only — it does NOT accept `source`/`data`; POST the
endpoint for those), `route.static.ts` for the no-DB site profile.

Custom forms (calculators, quote wizards) send two extra fields:
`source` (which form, ≤64 chars) and `data` (a JSON object of whatever you
collected, ≤50 keys / ≤4 KB). Anything outside `data` is stripped by Zod.
Over a cap = `400`, never a silent truncation.

Adding your OWN column to `Lead` needs `pnpm db:push` before the code runs:
Prisma names every column in `INSERT … RETURNING`, so an unmigrated database
fails on writes as well as reads.

## Don't

- Don't put credentials in tracked files (`.env.local`, `.mcp.json`, `i18nexus.json` are gitignored).
- Don't ship a new feature default-on without a feature flag.
- Don't rename `--color-*` CSS tokens once products reference them.
- Don't put JSON-LD inside `"use client"` components.
- Don't reach for a npm library when a Baseline-2024+ browser API does the same thing.

